    "question": {
      ":question/id": "43b3043d-dbf5-54dc-bf41-db8cd931ddfa",
      ":question/problem": "Find $s$, if $3{a}_{1}+s{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $s$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+s{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $s=4.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
